00:01:35.085 --> 00:01:35.960
So let's get started.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:39.790
I wanted to quickly review
what BeyondCorp really means

00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:41.540
and what it means for
you, the enterprise.

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:45.050
We think of this as really
Context-Aware secure access.

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:47.720
So as we dive into that, let
me start with a little bit of--

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:49.010
and this is the way
that Google did it.

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:50.420
I think this is the way
a lot of our customers

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:52.100
are going through
this model now.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:53.600
The traditional
model of security

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:55.910
is what we call the
Walled Garden approach.

00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:58.550
You have an employee who has to
access exciting and interesting

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:00.510
information and applications.

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:01.940
We make them go through a VPN.

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:03.350
No matter where
they sit, we make

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:05.900
them go through this tiresome,
cumbersome high-latency VPN,

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:08.840
and then they can get access
to all their applications.

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:11.840
That model starts to break down,
of course, as all of you know,

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:14.740
when you start to introduce
new users and new devices.

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:17.510
So you introduce a contractor,
who is not necessarily

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:19.490
a corporate employee,
and potentially

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:21.560
users that are coming
out on mobile devices.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:23.960
Getting to the applications
becomes harder and harder

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:24.566
in that model.

00:02:24.566 --> 00:02:25.940
And when you do
extend that model

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:28.121
to fit for contractors
and mobile devices,

00:02:28.121 --> 00:02:29.870
you find you have
unintended consequences.

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:32.286
Here, we have a contractor who
is supposed to be accessing

00:02:32.286 --> 00:02:34.481
the CRM system, but what
they're-- in essence,

00:02:34.481 --> 00:02:36.230
you're allowing them
to access everything,

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:37.410
including the ERP system.

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:41.240
So the Walled Garden gets
breached in this model.

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:43.370
The evolution of what
we're going through now

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:45.180
even more complicates
this model.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:47.060
So you're now taking these
applications that at least used

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:48.476
to be behind a
VPN, and now you're

00:02:48.476 --> 00:02:50.079
putting them in a Cloud--

00:02:50.079 --> 00:02:52.370
hopefully our Cloud, since
you're here, but many of you

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:53.720
are putting them in many Clouds.

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:55.790
And so now the security
model and access model

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:59.340
starts to really break down
on how this world works.

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:00.890
And then finally,
what you've moved--

00:03:00.890 --> 00:03:02.810
as some of our leading
customers are starting to do--

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:04.929
is start to move identity
into the cloud as well.

00:03:04.929 --> 00:03:06.470
So there's no more
on-prem AD or it's

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:08.209
becoming a secondary thing.

00:03:08.209 --> 00:03:10.500
Well, really, now, identity
lives in the Cloud as well.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:12.416
So everything, in essence,
is on the internet.

00:03:12.416 --> 00:03:14.720
You're in multiple places
with multiple applications.

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:16.430
Securing access to
all of this world

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:19.500
becomes very, very complicated.

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:21.620
And so what that does
is, it introduces

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:22.929
a whole set of new risk.

00:03:22.929 --> 00:03:24.470
There's many, many
here, but I wanted

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:26.370
to highlight some
of the key ones.

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:28.220
And I think this is
the key takeaway slide.

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:30.886
There are lots of things that we
start to introduce as problems.

00:03:30.886 --> 00:03:33.740
And your vendors or you need
to start introducing solutions

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:34.699
for these problems.

00:03:34.699 --> 00:03:35.990
So the first one, let's start--

00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:37.823
I mean, we can start
at the very beginning--

00:03:37.823 --> 00:03:39.912
the employee,
themselves, are they

00:03:39.912 --> 00:03:41.120
really who they say they are?

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:42.440
Are they using the
right credentials

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:43.680
or has someone stolen
those credentials?

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:45.440
You think OTP's are
safe, but those are just

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:46.981
bearer tokens in
essence, so somebody

00:03:46.981 --> 00:03:48.851
can come steal that
and use that as well.

00:03:48.851 --> 00:03:50.600
So now you have to
worry, is this the user

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:52.160
that they say they are?

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:53.975
Even if the user is
who they say they are,

00:03:53.975 --> 00:03:55.850
if they're using a device
that's compromised,

00:03:55.850 --> 00:03:58.224
they're using a personal device
that has a lot of malware

00:03:58.224 --> 00:04:01.370
on it, what you're finding is
that whatever data is accessed,

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:04.640
the device has access
to, is now stolen data.

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:07.220
And we'll talk about this
in a bit more detail.

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:09.170
I think a big and
complicated part of all this

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:10.430
is, of course, the network.

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:12.189
One is, is your
network encrypted?

00:04:12.189 --> 00:04:13.730
Is there a passive
listener on there?

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:15.980
Is there a man in the middle
that's happening here?

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:17.694
Can your network
handle a Dos attack?

00:04:17.694 --> 00:04:19.610
Can it handle the load
that needs to be there?

00:04:19.610 --> 00:04:21.320
You have uptown requirements.

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:24.214
The network need to be able
to handle all of these things.

00:04:24.214 --> 00:04:26.630
And then, as we've talked
about, as you move to the Cloud,

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:29.600
the new model is, who can
see these applications?

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:31.987
Think about all the legacy,
lift-and-shift applications

00:04:31.987 --> 00:04:33.320
that you're moving to the Cloud.

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:35.530
They used to be safe
behind the walls of a VPN.

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:38.300
When you move them to the
Cloud, these legacy systems,

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:40.120
they don't have
traditional access models.

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:41.328
And now they're on the Cloud.

00:04:41.328 --> 00:04:43.490
Are they accessible by anybody?

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:46.130
And even if it's not a
lift-and-shift application,

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:48.140
applications that are
custom-built for the web--

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:49.760
and actually, Dale
has a good point,

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:51.343
so I'm not going to
steal its thunder.

00:04:51.343 --> 00:04:54.180
But you are very good
at application logic.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:56.180
Your developer's very
good at application logic.

00:04:56.180 --> 00:04:57.950
Is security necessarily
a strong point

00:04:57.950 --> 00:04:59.970
for what that person does?

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:03.600
And so we need to provide tools
and things in that domain.

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:05.660
So in essence, this is
a high-level overview

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:08.010
of where things
start to go wrong.

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:09.660
And installing it,
then, the right--

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:11.420
OK, good.

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:13.912
So then what you're
starting to see is--

00:05:13.912 --> 00:05:16.370
let's get concrete about the
things that I've talked about,

00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:17.622
like phishing and--

00:05:17.622 --> 00:05:19.580
I'll steal Christiaan's
thunder here-- phishing

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:21.288
becomes one of the
largest attack vectors

00:05:21.288 --> 00:05:23.390
against your organization.

00:05:23.390 --> 00:05:26.275
Somebody can either just fake
a website for your employee

00:05:26.275 --> 00:05:28.400
to go to and enter their
credentials and that's it,

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:31.610
or someone steals a token
or cookie from your browser.

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:34.360
All it takes is for
that token to be gone.

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:35.910
It's a bearer token
in essence now,

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:37.580
and your account is phished.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:39.560
Everyone can access as you.

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:40.670
The second one is malware.

00:05:40.670 --> 00:05:42.410
We see it in a lot
of our customers.

00:05:42.410 --> 00:05:43.670
They're saying, oh,
I had a backdoor

00:05:43.670 --> 00:05:44.870
I didn't even know about.

00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:47.376
Information just
started leaking around.

00:05:47.376 --> 00:05:49.250
The man-in-the-middle--
think of an example--

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:51.440
actually, we had a customer
that actually had this problem.

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:53.231
During Heartbleed,
somebody used Heartbleed

00:05:53.231 --> 00:05:56.930
to get search from a
supposedly protected server.

00:05:56.930 --> 00:05:59.480
Even though the company
thought the traffic was all

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:03.492
encrypted in HTTPs, the
key is out there now.

00:06:03.492 --> 00:06:05.450
People are listening
man-in-the-middle of those

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:06.170
attacks.

00:06:06.170 --> 00:06:08.266
Or all of you know
companies that

00:06:08.266 --> 00:06:09.640
have suffered
through Dos attacks

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:12.380
and have really suffered
through all those things.

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:15.420
The access policy-- access
policy is, in some points,

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:16.430
a choke point.

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:19.886
You can start to really
enforce access at these thing.

00:06:19.886 --> 00:06:22.010
And we'll talk about what
we can do with proxies.

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:23.635
And of course, on
the application side,

00:06:23.635 --> 00:06:26.589
it's very clear that you can
have cross-site scripting

00:06:26.589 --> 00:06:28.880
attacks, you can have buffer
overflows, all these types

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:30.963
of things that just end
up leaking information out

00:06:30.963 --> 00:06:32.090
into the world.

00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:34.580
And so there are solutions
to these problems.

00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:36.620
And there are lots of
players in this space,

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:38.420
but the vendors you go
with need to provide

00:06:38.420 --> 00:06:39.980
those kinds of solutions.

00:06:39.980 --> 00:06:42.242
You have to have some sort
of phishing-resistant auth,

00:06:42.242 --> 00:06:44.450
and we have a Security Key
solution, which Christiaan

00:06:44.450 --> 00:06:45.767
will talk to in a moment here.

00:06:45.767 --> 00:06:48.350
But the idea should be that even
if your username and password

00:06:48.350 --> 00:06:50.180
gets compromised, there
are other factors,

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:52.220
other second factors,
that are not compromisable

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:55.550
and they'll protect access
to your applications.

00:06:55.550 --> 00:06:57.589
Device management
is another key area

00:06:57.589 --> 00:06:59.630
where there's a lot of
opportunity for protection

00:06:59.630 --> 00:07:01.710
here, in two ways, if
you think about it.

00:07:01.710 --> 00:07:03.890
One is in the configuration
of your device.

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:05.690
And I'm enforcing
that anti-virus

00:07:05.690 --> 00:07:06.905
needs to be on this device.

00:07:06.905 --> 00:07:08.780
I need to make sure that
anti-virus is there,

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:11.070
for example, or in the
state of the device.

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:13.070
Even if everything seems
like it's set properly,

00:07:13.070 --> 00:07:14.960
is there a screen lock enabled?

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:16.370
Is it in the right IP address?

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:17.828
Those are the kinds
of things where

00:07:17.828 --> 00:07:20.000
you need to be able
to say, even if it's

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:21.920
the right person on a
corporate-issued device,

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:25.560
is the device in a good
state before I allow access?

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:29.090
The other part of this
layered cake, if you will,

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:31.870
is this idea that you
have well-configured TLS.

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:34.600
Managing the search for all
of the TLS and everything

00:07:34.600 --> 00:07:35.407
is a hard job.

00:07:35.407 --> 00:07:36.990
And a lot of DevOps
people are focused

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:38.460
on keeping everything
up, but not

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:40.590
necessarily in
managing these certs,

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:42.885
protecting against DoS
attacks, and things like that.

00:07:42.885 --> 00:07:43.920
That needs to be--

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:46.420
we need to make sure that we
offer those solutions right out

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:47.250
of the box for you.

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:49.209
To me, the most interesting
part of this talk,

00:07:49.209 --> 00:07:51.000
because it's one of
the products I work on,

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:54.210
is this idea that you have
a proxy choke point that

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:55.350
controls access.

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:57.510
You can do a lot of
things at this layer.

00:07:57.510 --> 00:07:58.968
The first thing
that you can do is,

00:07:58.968 --> 00:08:01.010
you can start to take the
context of the request.

00:08:01.010 --> 00:08:02.634
Is it coming from
the right IP address?

00:08:02.634 --> 00:08:03.980
Is it coming from a safe device?

00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:06.120
Was the user strong
authed in all of this?

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:08.610
And say, yes, all of
these boxes check.

00:08:08.610 --> 00:08:10.950
Let's allow access
to go on here.

00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:13.140
But the proxy also
does other things.

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:17.355
It says, unlike a VPN model
in the old world, you can say,

00:08:17.355 --> 00:08:19.260
now you can create more
fine-grained access

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:20.630
control over these places.

00:08:20.630 --> 00:08:23.235
The ERP app is out-of-bounds
for me, the CRM app isn't.

00:08:23.235 --> 00:08:25.360
You can start to set those
types of access policies

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:25.944
at this proxy.

00:08:25.944 --> 00:08:28.068
And then, of course, you
should always continuously

00:08:28.068 --> 00:08:29.527
be doing scans of
your applications

00:08:29.527 --> 00:08:31.276
that are running in
the Cloud, making sure

00:08:31.276 --> 00:08:33.510
that there's no security
vulnerabilities or anything

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:35.450
that's gone wild out there.

00:08:35.450 --> 00:08:39.090
And so we envision a world
where an administrator

00:08:39.090 --> 00:08:41.789
or a developer, even, can
start to set very simplified

00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:44.370
policies that have very
powerful consequences.

00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:47.760
An example of that is, for
access to my ERP application,

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:50.344
I only want members of my
finance group to access this.

00:08:50.344 --> 00:08:52.260
No setting up VPNs, no
making sure everybody's

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:53.220
at the right place.

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:56.670
Only members of my finance team
should be able to have access.

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:58.590
They should only be
coming from corp devices.

00:08:58.590 --> 00:09:00.840
They should only be coming
from in [INAUDIBLE] network

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:02.370
or from the right geographies.

00:09:02.370 --> 00:09:04.640
I want to make sure they
are who they say they are,

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:05.550
and I want to make
sure that that's

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:07.170
coming across a secure network.

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:09.140
Those are very key policies
that I want to set.

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:10.890
They should be check,
check, check, check.

00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:12.990
And you should be getting
that out of the box.

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:15.270
Google Cloud, especially
after the announcements

00:09:15.270 --> 00:09:18.000
we made yesterday, if any
of you saw Urs's talk,

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:20.340
starts to offer a lot of
solutions in this space.

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:22.420
We introduced the
identity where proxy, this

00:09:22.420 --> 00:09:24.420
is how we want to control
access to applications

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:28.565
by users' identities from
well-managed devices.

00:09:28.565 --> 00:09:29.940
Watch the things
that we're going

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:31.023
to start to be doing here.

00:09:31.023 --> 00:09:32.570
We have an MDM solution already.

00:09:32.570 --> 00:09:35.570
We're going to offer richer
solutions coming out shortly.

00:09:35.570 --> 00:09:37.140
From certain
geographies, we want

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:39.098
to make sure you can
start to set policies that

00:09:39.098 --> 00:09:40.620
say corp network,
not corp network,

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:42.995
not from sanctioned countries,
from sanctioned countries,

00:09:42.995 --> 00:09:44.560
whatever it happens to be.

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:46.917
We also want you to be able
to use Security Key to be--

00:09:46.917 --> 00:09:49.500
I know I'm not supposed to use
the word unphishable-- but very

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:53.097
phishing-resistant in
accessing these applications.

00:09:53.097 --> 00:09:55.430
And the load balancer starts
to provide a lot of things.

00:09:55.430 --> 00:09:56.940
It provides your
TLS terminations.

00:09:56.940 --> 00:09:59.315
You want to be able to be
hardened against these attacks.

00:09:59.315 --> 00:10:02.010
We offer solutions
in these spaces.

00:10:02.010 --> 00:10:05.450
So a lot of these things
we already provide here.

00:10:05.450 --> 00:10:07.070
You see the green
and white boxes.

00:10:07.070 --> 00:10:10.062
What we're now introducing
today, or actually yesterday,

00:10:10.062 --> 00:10:12.020
are two new parts of this
chain, which we think

00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:13.690
gives you full coverage
from end to end.

00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:15.356
And it's not that the
journey ever ends,

00:10:15.356 --> 00:10:17.690
but now we think we have
a lot of the boxes checked

00:10:17.690 --> 00:10:19.130
in total security.

00:10:19.130 --> 00:10:20.630
First is Security
Key enforcement,

00:10:20.630 --> 00:10:22.645
and the second is
Identity-Aware Proxy.

00:10:22.645 --> 00:10:24.770
And so we'll talk about
these two products briefly,

00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:26.540
and then we'll let Dale
come up on afterwards

00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:28.206
and talk through how
these things become

00:10:28.206 --> 00:10:30.120
actually useful in the wild.

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:31.970
So I'll start with the
Identity-Aware Proxy

00:10:31.970 --> 00:10:33.470
and then hand it
over to Christiaan.

00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:36.440
We call it IAP for
its loving name, which

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:37.790
is this part of the cycle.

00:10:37.790 --> 00:10:40.940
IAP, actually, the
idea is very simple,

00:10:40.940 --> 00:10:43.670
but the value it provides
is actually quite huge here.

00:10:43.670 --> 00:10:46.100
What we want to do is, we
want to give administrators

00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:48.170
and developers a way
of configuring access

00:10:48.170 --> 00:10:50.470
controls by the people who
are supposed to get access

00:10:50.470 --> 00:10:52.370
to those applications.

00:10:52.370 --> 00:10:54.029
You set users, you
set the groups,

00:10:54.029 --> 00:10:56.320
you set the application that
it's supposed to reach to.

00:10:56.320 --> 00:10:58.610
And IAP enforces
that access control.

00:10:58.610 --> 00:11:01.820
And the value here
is that the devs

00:11:01.820 --> 00:11:04.019
can focus on the logic
of their applications.

00:11:04.019 --> 00:11:06.560
We take care of all the other
things that go along and around

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:07.435
the security of that.

00:11:07.435 --> 00:11:10.040
And the administrator has
full control in that world.

00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:12.620
And so the experience is
actually very simplified here.

00:11:12.620 --> 00:11:14.450
The administrator comes in--

00:11:14.450 --> 00:11:16.130
this is not actual
policy syntax,

00:11:16.130 --> 00:11:17.750
but I wanted to
use it to highlight

00:11:17.750 --> 00:11:19.820
how things work here--

00:11:19.820 --> 00:11:22.100
you specify very clearly,
members of the finance team

00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:23.514
can access my ERP application.

00:11:23.514 --> 00:11:24.930
That's it, it's
as simple as that.

00:11:24.930 --> 00:11:26.990
And if any of you have
seen the UI, you can see,

00:11:26.990 --> 00:11:29.660
it's actually literally where
you pick your application back

00:11:29.660 --> 00:11:32.057
in, you click the slider to
activate, add your users,

00:11:32.057 --> 00:11:33.140
and you're up and running.

00:11:33.140 --> 00:11:34.800
It's that quick.

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:37.622
So then the next day, the
user actually comes along.

00:11:37.622 --> 00:11:38.330
Here comes Sarah.

00:11:38.330 --> 00:11:41.270
She's a member of the finance
group, we can validate that.

00:11:41.270 --> 00:11:42.382
Actually, let me go back.

00:11:42.382 --> 00:11:44.090
And she is, in fact,
who she says she is.

00:11:44.090 --> 00:11:46.090
If we don't know who she
is, we [INAUDIBLE] send

00:11:46.090 --> 00:11:47.916
her back to your SSE
or IDP to validate,

00:11:47.916 --> 00:11:49.790
or if it's a Google
identity, we can send her

00:11:49.790 --> 00:11:51.040
to the Google Identity system.

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:53.170
She validates who she is.

00:11:53.170 --> 00:11:55.550
Once IAP does that check and
says, yes, this is, in fact,

00:11:55.550 --> 00:11:58.880
Sarah, yes, she actually
belongs to the IAP--

00:11:58.880 --> 00:12:00.840
she actually belongs
to the finance team,

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:03.212
the next thing to do
is, does she get access?

00:12:03.212 --> 00:12:04.920
IAP makes that check
and says, yes, Sarah

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:08.040
does, in fact, get access
to the ERP application.

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:09.530
And so she's in
and she's running,

00:12:09.530 --> 00:12:12.260
and her life should go on
very smoothly and easily.

00:12:12.260 --> 00:12:14.540
And we avoid the unintended
access consequence, which

00:12:14.540 --> 00:12:16.377
is to say, but she
hasn't been specified

00:12:16.377 --> 00:12:18.710
to access the CRM application,
so she doesn't get access

00:12:18.710 --> 00:12:19.760
to the CRM application.

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:21.350
It's as simple as that.

00:12:21.350 --> 00:12:22.820
And so that is,
in essence, how we

00:12:22.820 --> 00:12:26.370
want to deliver IAP as a first
step of the BeyondCorp vision

00:12:26.370 --> 00:12:28.864
to a lot of our customers
that are running in the Cloud.

00:12:28.864 --> 00:12:30.530
And I think, as the
second part of that,

00:12:30.530 --> 00:12:32.655
Christiaan will come up
and talk about strong auth.

00:12:39.062 --> 00:12:40.770
CHRISTIAAN BRAND:
Hello, can you hear me?

00:12:40.770 --> 00:12:41.503
I'm on?

00:12:41.503 --> 00:12:42.003
Yep.

00:12:42.003 --> 00:12:42.710
Hi.

00:12:42.710 --> 00:12:44.990
Hi everyone, I'm
Christiaan Brand.

00:12:44.990 --> 00:12:46.370
Thanks Ameet.

00:12:46.370 --> 00:12:48.440
I'm a product manager
on the team at Google

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:52.190
responsible for
identity and security.

00:12:52.190 --> 00:12:53.690
And over the next
couple of minutes,

00:12:53.690 --> 00:12:57.540
I really want to be focusing
on secure user authentication--

00:12:57.540 --> 00:12:59.690
that's the green part there--

00:12:59.690 --> 00:13:03.260
and how we can make sure
that we're providing access

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:06.740
in this model to
the rightful user.

00:13:06.740 --> 00:13:09.620
So phishing-- and I'm sure you
all know exactly what phishing

00:13:09.620 --> 00:13:11.340
is, but just to recap briefly--

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:14.090
phishing, an attack where
a bad guy tricks a user

00:13:14.090 --> 00:13:17.350
into revealing their sensitive
credentials, such as a user

00:13:17.350 --> 00:13:20.030
name and a password,
to the wrong website,

00:13:20.030 --> 00:13:23.900
accounted for 2/3 of
the security incidents

00:13:23.900 --> 00:13:27.855
in 2015, according to a
Verizon data breach report.

00:13:27.855 --> 00:13:29.480
Now, other than
phishing, there is also

00:13:29.480 --> 00:13:31.340
other types of compromises--

00:13:31.340 --> 00:13:33.980
passwords get
compromised through reuse

00:13:33.980 --> 00:13:35.810
and interception--

00:13:35.810 --> 00:13:38.130
but the industry has a
solution to this problem.

00:13:38.130 --> 00:13:41.030
The industry solution is
multi-factor authentication.

00:13:41.030 --> 00:13:43.730
But the problem is that most of
the multi-factor authentication

00:13:43.730 --> 00:13:48.230
solutions today rely on just
another form of a password,

00:13:48.230 --> 00:13:50.030
the one-time password.

00:13:50.030 --> 00:13:52.340
And these really are
just as trivially

00:13:52.340 --> 00:13:56.100
easy to phish as the
user's standard password.

00:13:56.100 --> 00:13:58.760
And it doesn't really
provide adequate protection

00:13:58.760 --> 00:14:01.160
for high-value accounts.

00:14:01.160 --> 00:14:04.100
So Google's research
have also shown that even

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:06.740
with all the user education
going on and trying

00:14:06.740 --> 00:14:10.520
to warn users against phishing
attacks and educate them,

00:14:10.520 --> 00:14:14.420
users will still fall for
sophisticated phishing scams

00:14:14.420 --> 00:14:15.660
about half of the time.

00:14:15.660 --> 00:14:19.730
So this really is a
systemic industry problem.

00:14:19.730 --> 00:14:22.430
So the mechanism
we use at Google

00:14:22.430 --> 00:14:25.640
to achieve secure and
phishing-resistant logins

00:14:25.640 --> 00:14:28.010
is based on a tiny
piece of hardware

00:14:28.010 --> 00:14:29.790
we call the Security Key.

00:14:29.790 --> 00:14:31.040
You can see that on the right.

00:14:31.040 --> 00:14:34.520
So Security Keys form the
basis of all authentication

00:14:34.520 --> 00:14:35.750
at Google.

00:14:35.750 --> 00:14:38.430
And if you use Google as
your identity provider,

00:14:38.430 --> 00:14:42.950
you can also benefit from
this same advanced protection.

00:14:42.950 --> 00:14:45.800
So let's briefly take
a couple of moments

00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:49.790
here and go through how Security
Keys really work and achieve

00:14:49.790 --> 00:14:54.470
this elevated level of
protection against phishing.

00:14:54.470 --> 00:14:57.740
So typically, if we look at
what we're presenting here,

00:14:57.740 --> 00:14:59.300
there is a user,
the user is trying

00:14:59.300 --> 00:15:00.590
to get access to a server.

00:15:00.590 --> 00:15:02.870
What normally happens in
single-factor authentication

00:15:02.870 --> 00:15:05.930
world, a password gets sent
from the left to the right,

00:15:05.930 --> 00:15:08.570
and that provides the user
access to the resource,

00:15:08.570 --> 00:15:09.900
pretty simple.

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:12.596
So let's take a look at
these next few slides.

00:15:12.596 --> 00:15:14.220
So we see something
on the screen here,

00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:16.340
and I'm going to show
you something else now.

00:15:16.340 --> 00:15:16.989
What changed?

00:15:16.989 --> 00:15:19.280
Does anyone have an idea what
changed between these two

00:15:19.280 --> 00:15:21.220
slides?

00:15:21.220 --> 00:15:22.690
So let me call that out.

00:15:22.690 --> 00:15:25.300
The URL bar, that's
phishing, right?

00:15:25.300 --> 00:15:28.052
So a user thinks they're
on exactly the right site,

00:15:28.052 --> 00:15:29.760
they're going to input
their credentials,

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:33.330
but actually, they're not
really on the rightful website.

00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:35.170
And because users,
we're trained to look

00:15:35.170 --> 00:15:36.770
at the URL bar and
things like that,

00:15:36.770 --> 00:15:38.680
but it's a lot of
cognitive load for users.

00:15:38.680 --> 00:15:40.720
And most of the
time, they actually--

00:15:40.720 --> 00:15:43.450
like we said with our
research as proven--

00:15:43.450 --> 00:15:45.700
about half of the
time, users actually

00:15:45.700 --> 00:15:49.390
don't check for all these
little details on the pages.

00:15:49.390 --> 00:15:52.090
And they still actually
end up in these traps

00:15:52.090 --> 00:15:54.787
that a malicious bad
guy set for them,

00:15:54.787 --> 00:15:57.120
and they give away credentials
to some phishing website.

00:15:57.120 --> 00:15:59.590
Now, one of the questions
is, how did they

00:15:59.590 --> 00:16:01.480
get to this page
in the first place?

00:16:01.480 --> 00:16:02.560
They didn't just mistype.

00:16:05.696 --> 00:16:07.570
And I'll show that in
a second, you're right.

00:16:07.570 --> 00:16:09.430
So typically, let's just talk
through the phishing attack,

00:16:09.430 --> 00:16:10.060
pretty simple.

00:16:10.060 --> 00:16:13.870
User enters password,
password goes to bad guy,

00:16:13.870 --> 00:16:16.330
bad guy sends password
to rightful server,

00:16:16.330 --> 00:16:18.890
and bad guy's logged in,
pretty straightforward.

00:16:18.890 --> 00:16:21.280
So let's look at the next
slide and how the user actually

00:16:21.280 --> 00:16:23.690
gets to this bad website
in the first place.

00:16:23.690 --> 00:16:26.440
Well, normally,
that's the model.

00:16:26.440 --> 00:16:28.810
Someone get sent
an email, the email

00:16:28.810 --> 00:16:31.690
looks like it's from
the right party.

00:16:31.690 --> 00:16:33.967
The email might say
something like, hey, you

00:16:33.967 --> 00:16:36.550
need to immediately change your
password because someone tried

00:16:36.550 --> 00:16:37.840
to log in to your account.

00:16:37.840 --> 00:16:39.910
The user would
click on the link,

00:16:39.910 --> 00:16:44.470
unknowingly going to a phishing
website, because there is--

00:16:44.470 --> 00:16:47.770
it's hard for the user to figure
out whether this email really

00:16:47.770 --> 00:16:49.062
came from a trusted source.

00:16:49.062 --> 00:16:50.770
And there's other
protections, of course,

00:16:50.770 --> 00:16:51.490
that's in place there.

00:16:51.490 --> 00:16:53.590
But at the bare basic
level, users [INAUDIBLE]

00:16:53.590 --> 00:16:55.090
still fall for these attacks.

00:16:55.090 --> 00:16:56.920
And that's what we see.

00:16:56.920 --> 00:16:59.945
Like I said, about 2/3
of incidents in 2015

00:16:59.945 --> 00:17:01.570
was as a result of
this kind of attack.

00:17:01.570 --> 00:17:03.778
So we really need to do
something to try and protect.

00:17:03.778 --> 00:17:06.310
So let's now look at
Security Keys very briefly.

00:17:06.310 --> 00:17:09.670
So what we have is a
user, here on the left.

00:17:09.670 --> 00:17:12.849
The user is trying to
sign in to a website.

00:17:12.849 --> 00:17:15.730
But in addition to only
sending the password,

00:17:15.730 --> 00:17:17.200
something else happens here.

00:17:17.200 --> 00:17:20.560
The user typically
has a Security Key.

00:17:20.560 --> 00:17:23.750
Security Keys come in various
different form factors.

00:17:23.750 --> 00:17:27.130
We have USB-based Security Keys,
we've had them for a while.

00:17:27.130 --> 00:17:28.870
We now also have
Security Keys that

00:17:28.870 --> 00:17:31.720
can be attached over NFC,
near-field communication,

00:17:31.720 --> 00:17:34.510
and Bluetooth, Bluetooth
Low Energy, or BLE.

00:17:34.510 --> 00:17:36.690
So this Security Key
on the left-hand side

00:17:36.690 --> 00:17:38.800
is with the user when
they try to sign in,

00:17:38.800 --> 00:17:42.370
but this Security Key also
contain a cryptographic key.

00:17:42.370 --> 00:17:44.530
It contains a
unique, private key.

00:17:44.530 --> 00:17:47.170
And there is an associated
key at the server

00:17:47.170 --> 00:17:48.440
called the public key.

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:50.500
And this kind of key
pair got generated

00:17:50.500 --> 00:17:52.870
at some point in the past
when the user registered

00:17:52.870 --> 00:17:55.002
their access for this
specific service.

00:17:55.002 --> 00:17:56.710
When their password
was set up, they also

00:17:56.710 --> 00:17:58.390
registered their
Security Key, which

00:17:58.390 --> 00:17:59.890
created these two key pairs.

00:17:59.890 --> 00:18:01.840
And the public key got
sent to the server,

00:18:01.840 --> 00:18:03.910
and the private key got
generated and stored

00:18:03.910 --> 00:18:05.030
on the token.

00:18:05.030 --> 00:18:07.330
So what happens now, when
the user tries to sign in,

00:18:07.330 --> 00:18:09.370
there is actually a
password that goes down.

00:18:09.370 --> 00:18:10.930
Yes, that's your first factor.

00:18:10.930 --> 00:18:13.600
But there's also an
additional signed--

00:18:13.600 --> 00:18:16.030
very important--
cryptographic assertion

00:18:16.030 --> 00:18:18.880
that goes from the
device to the server.

00:18:18.880 --> 00:18:21.670
And this cryptographic
assertion has three

00:18:21.670 --> 00:18:23.540
very important components.

00:18:23.540 --> 00:18:26.410
So the first one is the
promise that the user

00:18:26.410 --> 00:18:27.850
was physically present.

00:18:27.850 --> 00:18:31.570
And the way we promise
that is by simply, actually

00:18:31.570 --> 00:18:33.190
allowing the user
to only generate

00:18:33.190 --> 00:18:35.650
the insertion of the device
to only generate the insertion

00:18:35.650 --> 00:18:37.734
when the button was
clicked on the token.

00:18:37.734 --> 00:18:39.400
So you'll see, with
these Security Keys,

00:18:39.400 --> 00:18:42.310
they normally have a
capacitive touch or resistive

00:18:42.310 --> 00:18:44.530
touch, or a physical button
that need to be pressed.

00:18:44.530 --> 00:18:46.450
So at the point in time
where you want to sign in,

00:18:46.450 --> 00:18:48.220
you need to physically
press this button.

00:18:48.220 --> 00:18:49.960
So that's the
promise, that a user

00:18:49.960 --> 00:18:53.040
was present at the time
of log in at the machine.

00:18:53.040 --> 00:18:54.700
There's a server challenge.

00:18:54.700 --> 00:18:56.950
And this is just part of
the usual crypto response

00:18:56.950 --> 00:18:58.500
to protect against
replay attacks.

00:18:58.500 --> 00:19:00.730
So the server sends a
challenge to the device,

00:19:00.730 --> 00:19:04.390
and then the token responds back
with that specific challenge,

00:19:04.390 --> 00:19:05.954
so in the response.

00:19:05.954 --> 00:19:07.370
But that's not the
most important.

00:19:07.370 --> 00:19:09.310
The most important part
of this, and the thing

00:19:09.310 --> 00:19:12.450
that really protects the user
against phishing at this point,

00:19:12.450 --> 00:19:15.380
is that third line there
that we call the origin.

00:19:15.380 --> 00:19:18.089
So what happens is, when the
user is on a specific website

00:19:18.089 --> 00:19:20.380
on the left-hand side here,
or they're trying to access

00:19:20.380 --> 00:19:25.340
a certain service, the
URI and the specific eTLD,

00:19:25.340 --> 00:19:28.820
or the top-level
domain, normally +1,

00:19:28.820 --> 00:19:31.690
in the case of the
protocol that I have here,

00:19:31.690 --> 00:19:35.080
that's specified by
the specific request.

00:19:35.080 --> 00:19:38.110
But when the user is on
a website, that website,

00:19:38.110 --> 00:19:40.510
for lack of a better term,
that they're on actually

00:19:40.510 --> 00:19:42.640
gets incorporated in
the digital signature

00:19:42.640 --> 00:19:45.290
that goes back to the server.

00:19:45.290 --> 00:19:47.830
So whatever the user is
on on the left-hand side

00:19:47.830 --> 00:19:49.540
gets incorporated
into the signature

00:19:49.540 --> 00:19:50.710
and sent to the server.

00:19:50.710 --> 00:19:52.870
So if the user is on the
rightful Google website,

00:19:52.870 --> 00:19:54.400
the Google server on
the right-hand side

00:19:54.400 --> 00:19:56.983
will look at the signature and
say, yep, that's what I expect.

00:19:56.983 --> 00:19:59.410
I expected the user to be
on this specific website.

00:19:59.410 --> 00:20:01.540
That's what the signature
presented to me,

00:20:01.540 --> 00:20:03.550
so I'll allow the
user to sign in.

00:20:03.550 --> 00:20:06.310
So let's look at
a phishing attack

00:20:06.310 --> 00:20:08.440
and how the Security
Key, in this case,

00:20:08.440 --> 00:20:11.230
defeat the phishing attack
that we saw a little earlier.

00:20:11.230 --> 00:20:12.515
So we have the same example.

00:20:12.515 --> 00:20:14.140
We've got the Security
Key on the left,

00:20:14.140 --> 00:20:16.570
we have the corresponding
public key on the right.

00:20:16.570 --> 00:20:18.190
We have the user
trying to sign in.

00:20:18.190 --> 00:20:20.080
There is a phishing
guy in the middle.

00:20:20.080 --> 00:20:22.080
User is the wrong
URL, as we can see.

00:20:22.080 --> 00:20:25.020
So what happens is, we send
the password, as per normal,

00:20:25.020 --> 00:20:26.490
over to the bad guy.

00:20:26.490 --> 00:20:30.510
The bad guy presents the
crypto challenge to the user.

00:20:30.510 --> 00:20:32.900
The user clicks the button
on their Security Key,

00:20:32.900 --> 00:20:34.620
a assertion gets generated.

00:20:34.620 --> 00:20:37.200
And the assertion that the
bad guy gets at this point

00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:40.350
is obviously an assertion
for the wrong website.

00:20:40.350 --> 00:20:42.390
So when the bad guy
sends that assertion back

00:20:42.390 --> 00:20:44.880
to the rightful relying
party or the server--

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:46.140
Google, in this case--

00:20:46.140 --> 00:20:47.560
Google will look
at that and say,

00:20:47.560 --> 00:20:50.849
hey, I don't expect this origin
to be goggle.com, I'm Google.

00:20:50.849 --> 00:20:53.390
And at that point in time, they
will simply reject the login.

00:20:53.390 --> 00:20:55.830
So it's really, actually
pretty straightforward

00:20:55.830 --> 00:20:57.400
if you break it up like this.

00:20:57.400 --> 00:21:01.020
But the point is, the bad guy
in the middle cannot tamper with

00:21:01.020 --> 00:21:03.690
that message block that
gets sent from the client

00:21:03.690 --> 00:21:07.650
to the server because it's
digitally signed using this

00:21:07.650 --> 00:21:11.370
private key that was generated
out of band previously.

00:21:11.370 --> 00:21:15.270
So that's the protocol
in general, so pretty

00:21:15.270 --> 00:21:18.270
straightforward, but
really pretty powerful.

00:21:18.270 --> 00:21:21.540
So another question is, how does
this technology apply to you?

00:21:21.540 --> 00:21:23.760
Well, we launched something
we call Security Key

00:21:23.760 --> 00:21:25.590
Enforcement about a month ago.

00:21:25.590 --> 00:21:27.310
We spoke about it
earlier as well.

00:21:27.310 --> 00:21:29.820
And this really allows
you, as an administrator,

00:21:29.820 --> 00:21:34.940
to enforce the use of Security
Keys on certain groups of users

00:21:34.940 --> 00:21:37.830
or in certain
organizational units.

00:21:37.830 --> 00:21:39.420
And by enforcing
Security Keys, you

00:21:39.420 --> 00:21:42.630
make it really hard for bad
guys to trick your users

00:21:42.630 --> 00:21:45.360
into falling for phishing scams,
because even if the user is

00:21:45.360 --> 00:21:47.457
on a website that
there shouldn't be on,

00:21:47.457 --> 00:21:49.290
the Security Key will
generate the signature

00:21:49.290 --> 00:21:52.809
that your server will be
able to detect as fraudulent.

00:21:52.809 --> 00:21:54.850
And the other thing is,
Security Keys, of course,

00:21:54.850 --> 00:21:57.870
now supported on both
desktop and mobile.

00:21:57.870 --> 00:22:00.100
We've had Security Key
support for a while at Google.

00:22:00.100 --> 00:22:02.490
It was mainly around
USB, but we now also

00:22:02.490 --> 00:22:05.280
have support on
Android and iOS devices

00:22:05.280 --> 00:22:09.840
using NFC and Bluetooth
protocols there.

00:22:09.840 --> 00:22:11.730
Then, turning it on
for a subset of users

00:22:11.730 --> 00:22:13.140
is really straightforward.

00:22:13.140 --> 00:22:15.600
Typically, it's a
one-click event.

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:17.470
Where you configure
two-step verification,

00:22:17.470 --> 00:22:18.970
you now have the
option to say, hey,

00:22:18.970 --> 00:22:21.139
I want only Secure Keys
on these groups of users.

00:22:21.139 --> 00:22:22.680
And at that point,
it'll be enforced.

00:22:22.680 --> 00:22:25.080
And all the other controls
that goes together with this--

00:22:25.080 --> 00:22:27.990
onboarding users, taking
them through the onboarding

00:22:27.990 --> 00:22:28.800
process--

00:22:28.800 --> 00:22:34.020
that's all part and parcel
of this one-click event.

00:22:34.020 --> 00:22:37.890
So as a last slide here,
Stripe, one of our customers,

00:22:37.890 --> 00:22:39.570
has rolled out Security
Key enforcement

00:22:39.570 --> 00:22:41.580
to all of their users.

00:22:41.580 --> 00:22:43.800
And they really believe
that this is the perfect way

00:22:43.800 --> 00:22:46.440
to balance ease of use
with increased account

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:51.220
security across both
desktop and mobile devices.

00:22:51.220 --> 00:22:54.180
So in summary, if you're
worried about phishing,

00:22:54.180 --> 00:22:55.949
implement Security
Key enforcement.

00:22:55.949 --> 00:22:57.990
And if you want controlled
access to applications

00:22:57.990 --> 00:23:02.010
from the internet, implement
Identity-Aware Proxy.

00:23:02.010 --> 00:23:05.160
And with that, let me hand
you over to Dale from Vendasta

00:23:05.160 --> 00:23:06.710
to talk about their
implementation.

00:23:06.710 --> 00:23:07.230
Thank you.

00:23:07.230 --> 00:23:09.692
DALE HOPKINS: Thank you.

00:23:09.692 --> 00:23:11.900
So hopefully, these slide
transitions work all right.

00:23:11.900 --> 00:23:13.358
So what I'm going
to start off with

00:23:13.358 --> 00:23:14.930
is the obligatory
marketing slide,

00:23:14.930 --> 00:23:17.690
but I think it's actually
pretty relevant, in this case,

00:23:17.690 --> 00:23:19.626
to understand who
our customers are.

00:23:19.626 --> 00:23:21.250
And so the way we
describe our business

00:23:21.250 --> 00:23:24.020
is, we strive to be the
number-one platform for selling

00:23:24.020 --> 00:23:26.910
digital solutions to small
business or local business.

00:23:26.910 --> 00:23:28.400
And so if you want
to think of who

00:23:28.400 --> 00:23:30.860
is the end user of our
software, in a lot of cases,

00:23:30.860 --> 00:23:31.830
it's a small business.

00:23:31.830 --> 00:23:35.510
And so this is not necessarily
the high-value target,

00:23:35.510 --> 00:23:38.510
but it is often, also,
sometimes a person

00:23:38.510 --> 00:23:40.135
that's not super
familiar with digital,

00:23:40.135 --> 00:23:42.509
because one of the things
we're helping these people with

00:23:42.509 --> 00:23:44.750
is basically a digital
transformation for them,

00:23:44.750 --> 00:23:47.236
to use the word that
I guess we use here.

00:23:47.236 --> 00:23:48.860
What we're talking
about is, these guys

00:23:48.860 --> 00:23:50.120
used to buy Yellow Page ads.

00:23:50.120 --> 00:23:52.750
They used to buy all these
traditional media and things

00:23:52.750 --> 00:23:53.250
like that.

00:23:53.250 --> 00:23:56.240
And they're looking for, I
want customers, basically,

00:23:56.240 --> 00:23:57.230
in my store.

00:23:57.230 --> 00:23:59.450
Or even better, some
of them want customers

00:23:59.450 --> 00:24:00.950
on a website they don't have.

00:24:00.950 --> 00:24:03.440
So we essentially help
to provide those tools

00:24:03.440 --> 00:24:04.220
to small business.

00:24:04.220 --> 00:24:06.290
And the way we do
that is, 85% of them

00:24:06.290 --> 00:24:08.330
purchase from an
actual, physical person

00:24:08.330 --> 00:24:09.680
that comes and calls on them.

00:24:09.680 --> 00:24:11.060
So we do this through resellers.

00:24:11.060 --> 00:24:14.630
So we have 1,700 brands that
actually have salespeople

00:24:14.630 --> 00:24:17.300
that represent our
software as their brand

00:24:17.300 --> 00:24:19.100
and then sell this
to small business.

00:24:19.100 --> 00:24:21.860
And so for us, security, we
consider it pretty important.

00:24:21.860 --> 00:24:23.660
And we consider
security, actually,

00:24:23.660 --> 00:24:26.750
as one of those features,
that it's like performance

00:24:26.750 --> 00:24:29.210
in a lot of ways, in that
you don't really know its a

00:24:29.210 --> 00:24:31.880
problem until you don't
have it, and then you

00:24:31.880 --> 00:24:34.560
realize that you actually did
want performance or security.

00:24:34.560 --> 00:24:38.120
But unlike performance, if
you don't deliver on security,

00:24:38.120 --> 00:24:40.310
it's pretty hard to
actually regain trust.

00:24:40.310 --> 00:24:41.900
Because if you
have a slow website

00:24:41.900 --> 00:24:44.108
and you improve the performance,
I mean, that's cool,

00:24:44.108 --> 00:24:45.740
your users are
probably OK with that.

00:24:45.740 --> 00:24:49.430
But if you lose all their data
or they use their bank password

00:24:49.430 --> 00:24:54.170
to log in to your website and
you lost it, that's a big deal.

00:24:54.170 --> 00:24:55.580
And so, I guess,
looking at this,

00:24:55.580 --> 00:24:57.281
when we read the
BeyondCorp paper,

00:24:57.281 --> 00:24:59.030
there's a couple of
things that we thought

00:24:59.030 --> 00:25:00.530
were pretty striking on it.

00:25:00.530 --> 00:25:02.810
And it actually put--

00:25:02.810 --> 00:25:05.060
you guys all said you'd heard
of the BeyondCorp paper,

00:25:05.060 --> 00:25:09.180
how many of you have actually
read the BeyondCorp paper?

00:25:09.180 --> 00:25:12.530
So I don't see a
ton of hands, so I

00:25:12.530 --> 00:25:14.770
thought I would give
a couple of key pieces

00:25:14.770 --> 00:25:15.770
to the BeyondCorp paper.

00:25:15.770 --> 00:25:17.700
And I know that we
talked earlier--

00:25:17.700 --> 00:25:20.930
Ameet was saying we wanted to
change a little bit about VPNs.

00:25:20.930 --> 00:25:23.780
And I mean, a lot of
people are thinking,

00:25:23.780 --> 00:25:26.600
we're switching to Clouds,
so VPNs are too complicated,

00:25:26.600 --> 00:25:28.880
and so that's why we
don't want to use VPNs.

00:25:28.880 --> 00:25:31.400
And I think that, I mean, we're
actually missing the point,

00:25:31.400 --> 00:25:33.140
if that's what you're thinking.

00:25:33.140 --> 00:25:37.200
In terms of VPNs, the real
issue is that on a VPN,

00:25:37.200 --> 00:25:39.830
it's more of a traditional,
hard candy approach,

00:25:39.830 --> 00:25:40.780
I think they call it.

00:25:40.780 --> 00:25:42.260
And so the idea is, once
you're on the network,

00:25:42.260 --> 00:25:43.689
you have access to everything.

00:25:43.689 --> 00:25:45.230
And so really, what
we're seeing here

00:25:45.230 --> 00:25:48.230
is, if your application
design involves a VPN,

00:25:48.230 --> 00:25:50.360
and it's for a new
application, you're

00:25:50.360 --> 00:25:51.920
probably thinking
about it wrong,

00:25:51.920 --> 00:25:53.510
because what you're
basically saying

00:25:53.510 --> 00:25:57.510
is that I want to protect
access to my entire application,

00:25:57.510 --> 00:26:01.469
and as long as I can do that,
I'm going to be OK on security.

00:26:01.469 --> 00:26:02.760
And the way that I think that--

00:26:02.760 --> 00:26:04.160
it's not in the
BeyondCorp paper,

00:26:04.160 --> 00:26:07.050
but it was something I actually
heard at DockerCon last year.

00:26:07.050 --> 00:26:09.320
I don't remember the
speaker to give him credit,

00:26:09.320 --> 00:26:11.420
but what he said
is that if you're

00:26:11.420 --> 00:26:14.660
thinking about
preventing hackers,

00:26:14.660 --> 00:26:16.640
you probably have
already lost the game.

00:26:16.640 --> 00:26:19.610
Really, the answer you want
to do is, when you get hacked,

00:26:19.610 --> 00:26:21.470
you want to minimize
the damage, and you

00:26:21.470 --> 00:26:24.020
want to make sure that other
things don't get hacked.

00:26:24.020 --> 00:26:26.840
Because again,
especially for us,

00:26:26.840 --> 00:26:28.940
in this picture that
Ameet was showing before,

00:26:28.940 --> 00:26:30.772
I don't have control
over their devices.

00:26:30.772 --> 00:26:32.480
I don't have control
over a lot of stuff,

00:26:32.480 --> 00:26:35.930
so my goal is to actually make
it so that if one of my users

00:26:35.930 --> 00:26:38.180
does actually get attacked,
they don't get access

00:26:38.180 --> 00:26:39.380
to other user's data.

00:26:39.380 --> 00:26:42.147
So we can minimize
damage in that way.

00:26:42.147 --> 00:26:43.730
And then, the other
part is to make it

00:26:43.730 --> 00:26:47.810
harder, because again, it is
unlikely that a nation state is

00:26:47.810 --> 00:26:51.650
choosing to attack one of
our small business customers

00:26:51.650 --> 00:26:54.470
who runs a hardware
shop in Delaware

00:26:54.470 --> 00:26:59.084
to access his information about
his advertising or website.

00:26:59.084 --> 00:27:00.500
But at the same
time, I don't want

00:27:00.500 --> 00:27:02.250
to trivialize this
security problem either

00:27:02.250 --> 00:27:03.842
because it still is important.

00:27:03.842 --> 00:27:05.300
And so for this,
what we talk about

00:27:05.300 --> 00:27:07.070
is essentially a service proxy.

00:27:07.070 --> 00:27:08.370
And this is--

00:27:08.370 --> 00:27:10.970
Ameet was talking a little bit
about this earlier in terms of,

00:27:10.970 --> 00:27:12.914
this is--

00:27:12.914 --> 00:27:14.330
I mean, we simplified
a little bit

00:27:14.330 --> 00:27:16.250
in terms of saying
that this is just IAP,

00:27:16.250 --> 00:27:17.780
and I'll get into
that in a second.

00:27:17.780 --> 00:27:20.150
But as an example, on
our Cloud that we run,

00:27:20.150 --> 00:27:22.880
our entire infrastructure
is on Google Cloud.

00:27:22.880 --> 00:27:25.760
So I don't have an
on-premise deployment,

00:27:25.760 --> 00:27:27.410
and I don't have another Cloud.

00:27:27.410 --> 00:27:29.360
So those are not the
reasons for me switching

00:27:29.360 --> 00:27:31.210
to this sort of deployment.

00:27:31.210 --> 00:27:35.090
I control access to
all of my microservices

00:27:35.090 --> 00:27:38.030
and my deployments
through a service proxy.

00:27:38.030 --> 00:27:40.040
So that actually means
that all of my services

00:27:40.040 --> 00:27:41.630
are publicly accessible.

00:27:41.630 --> 00:27:44.630
And so we don't have a VPN,
we don't have special networks

00:27:44.630 --> 00:27:45.660
and things like that.

00:27:45.660 --> 00:27:47.480
They're all accessible this way.

00:27:47.480 --> 00:27:50.630
And so what I do is, I use the
multi-factor authentication,

00:27:50.630 --> 00:27:53.510
specifically for access to GCP.

00:27:53.510 --> 00:27:56.090
And I do actually
use service accounts,

00:27:56.090 --> 00:28:00.860
and I encrypt my traffic
between all of my microservices.

00:28:00.860 --> 00:28:03.170
How many of you here have
a microservice architecture

00:28:03.170 --> 00:28:06.100
that you guys run?

00:28:06.100 --> 00:28:07.590
We got a few hands.

00:28:07.590 --> 00:28:09.136
So quite of you
probably have a--

00:28:09.136 --> 00:28:11.260
I guess, so a show of hands,
how many people, then,

00:28:11.260 --> 00:28:12.923
have a monolith that they run?

00:28:12.923 --> 00:28:13.705
OK.

00:28:13.705 --> 00:28:15.580
So then, we've got about
half the people here

00:28:15.580 --> 00:28:16.329
that run software.

00:28:18.740 --> 00:28:21.269
But anyways, in either
case, what you have is,

00:28:21.269 --> 00:28:22.810
you essentially--
even in a monolith,

00:28:22.810 --> 00:28:24.700
it's likely that you have
segments of your code

00:28:24.700 --> 00:28:25.574
that you're exposing.

00:28:25.574 --> 00:28:26.902
Maybe not as a--

00:28:26.902 --> 00:28:28.360
maybe you could be
pedantic and you

00:28:28.360 --> 00:28:29.901
don't want to call
it a microservice,

00:28:29.901 --> 00:28:31.450
but it is a separate
piece of code.

00:28:31.450 --> 00:28:33.200
What we try to think
of is that encrypting

00:28:33.200 --> 00:28:35.410
between those systems is
actually really important,

00:28:35.410 --> 00:28:37.210
and trying to make sure
that you have identities

00:28:37.210 --> 00:28:38.840
and everything else
like that, again,

00:28:38.840 --> 00:28:40.965
because then if your
software, like a vulnerability

00:28:40.965 --> 00:28:43.655
or somebody gains access that
they shouldn't, they can't just

00:28:43.655 --> 00:28:45.280
sit there and browse
your whole network

00:28:45.280 --> 00:28:46.321
and see all your traffic.

00:28:46.321 --> 00:28:48.130
And so for us, the
way we do that is,

00:28:48.130 --> 00:28:49.900
we just make it
publicly accessible.

00:28:49.900 --> 00:28:50.884
And so it's pretty--

00:28:50.884 --> 00:28:53.050
it makes everybody, all the
engineers and everybody,

00:28:53.050 --> 00:28:55.300
think about, wait a minute,
this is a public-facing service,

00:28:55.300 --> 00:28:56.800
I obviously have
to encrypt things,

00:28:56.800 --> 00:29:00.280
I obviously need identity, and I
can't trust that this is a VPN.

00:29:00.280 --> 00:29:02.950
So actually, it leads to
some better practices.

00:29:02.950 --> 00:29:05.642
And so what we
actually said is--

00:29:05.642 --> 00:29:07.350
how many of you would
consider yourselves

00:29:07.350 --> 00:29:11.140
that you're fairly well-versed,
like understanding security?

00:29:11.140 --> 00:29:13.500
And then, is there anybody
in the audience here

00:29:13.500 --> 00:29:15.750
that sells security
as their product?

00:29:15.750 --> 00:29:17.030
Like two other vendors.

00:29:17.030 --> 00:29:20.220
So the thing that we look
at is, we don't do that.

00:29:20.220 --> 00:29:22.480
And so for us, if you're
a vendor of security,

00:29:22.480 --> 00:29:24.870
the solution I'm talking
about, potentially, this

00:29:24.870 --> 00:29:27.270
is something that
you don't necessarily

00:29:27.270 --> 00:29:28.840
want to do what
I'm talking about.

00:29:28.840 --> 00:29:32.010
But what I say is, I'm going
to delegate identity to Google.

00:29:32.010 --> 00:29:33.870
It's a really hard problem.

00:29:33.870 --> 00:29:36.510
And when Christiaan was up here,
you hear him talking about,

00:29:36.510 --> 00:29:39.720
we've got Special Keys of
hardware, we've got signing,

00:29:39.720 --> 00:29:41.250
we're thinking about pshishing.

00:29:41.250 --> 00:29:43.110
And then I'm sitting
in the audience,

00:29:43.110 --> 00:29:44.818
and I'm like, this
sounds great, but what

00:29:44.818 --> 00:29:46.270
happens if somebody hacks DNS?

00:29:46.270 --> 00:29:47.100
I don't know.

00:29:47.100 --> 00:29:48.030
These are the kind
of questions that I

00:29:48.030 --> 00:29:50.280
don't want to have my engineers
really worrying about.

00:29:50.280 --> 00:29:52.950
And so what we actually do
is, we delegate our security

00:29:52.950 --> 00:29:53.790
to Google.

00:29:53.790 --> 00:29:56.070
And again, I don't delegate
all of my security.

00:29:56.070 --> 00:29:59.995
So then, what it becomes is, I
have to think about security.

00:29:59.995 --> 00:30:01.950
I do consider you need
to be, at some level,

00:30:01.950 --> 00:30:03.825
an expert on security
to understand,

00:30:03.825 --> 00:30:06.270
what is Google doing and
am I comfortable with that,

00:30:06.270 --> 00:30:09.000
and making sure that, I mean,
I'm covering the other stuff

00:30:09.000 --> 00:30:10.080
that Google doesn't.

00:30:10.080 --> 00:30:13.290
And so in terms of the
security, the words

00:30:13.290 --> 00:30:16.200
that I enjoy that Google gave
out-- and I don't think it's

00:30:16.200 --> 00:30:18.120
fair to give them
credit for the naming--

00:30:18.120 --> 00:30:20.940
but we finally switched
from authentication

00:30:20.940 --> 00:30:23.811
and authorization to identity
and access management.

00:30:23.811 --> 00:30:25.560
I'm suspecting it's
one of the other Cloud

00:30:25.560 --> 00:30:26.550
vendors that came up with that.

00:30:26.550 --> 00:30:28.883
But I think the idea is, it's
a really good distinction,

00:30:28.883 --> 00:30:31.159
because they don't
both start with "off,"

00:30:31.159 --> 00:30:32.700
and so it makes it
easier to actually

00:30:32.700 --> 00:30:34.241
have a conversation
because you don't

00:30:34.241 --> 00:30:36.750
have that cognitive load of,
which one are we talking about?

00:30:36.750 --> 00:30:39.030
So I delegate identity to
Google because it's probably

00:30:39.030 --> 00:30:40.092
the hardest part.

00:30:40.092 --> 00:30:41.550
And in terms of
your security, it's

00:30:41.550 --> 00:30:43.144
one of the most
important pieces,

00:30:43.144 --> 00:30:45.810
because if I have a really great
access management system that's

00:30:45.810 --> 00:30:49.950
very good at controlling whether
Ameet has access to documents

00:30:49.950 --> 00:30:52.124
or whether I have
access to documents,

00:30:52.124 --> 00:30:54.540
but the system actually has a
really hard time determining

00:30:54.540 --> 00:30:56.580
whether it's me or
Ameet accessing it,

00:30:56.580 --> 00:30:58.740
my security's a waste of time.

00:30:58.740 --> 00:31:00.210
So for us, we
think that identity

00:31:00.210 --> 00:31:02.280
is one of the core parts, that
just because you get it right

00:31:02.280 --> 00:31:04.560
doesn't mean you're secure,
but if you get it wrong,

00:31:04.560 --> 00:31:06.710
you're pretty much not secure.

00:31:06.710 --> 00:31:08.550
And so we consider
that as something we

00:31:08.550 --> 00:31:10.520
choose Google as
a partner for this

00:31:10.520 --> 00:31:12.540
to make these
things more secure.

00:31:12.540 --> 00:31:17.160
And so in terms of what we also
do is, at this point, my users,

00:31:17.160 --> 00:31:19.670
my small businesses,
they log in to my system,

00:31:19.670 --> 00:31:21.420
so I've got usernames
and passwords.

00:31:21.420 --> 00:31:23.040
And so what we're
doing is, we're

00:31:23.040 --> 00:31:24.390
switching to single sign-on.

00:31:24.390 --> 00:31:25.350
And so that means--

00:31:25.350 --> 00:31:27.780
everybody here has probably
used that, in terms of--

00:31:27.780 --> 00:31:30.480
single sign-on is one of those
terms that gets thrown around

00:31:30.480 --> 00:31:33.690
and oftentimes abused,
but what it actually--

00:31:33.690 --> 00:31:37.680
I think the term, if you look
it up in its original version,

00:31:37.680 --> 00:31:39.625
what it's talking about
is, sign-in with Gmail

00:31:39.625 --> 00:31:41.250
would be an easy way
to think about it,

00:31:41.250 --> 00:31:43.650
or sign-in with Facebook,
or sign-in with GitHub,

00:31:43.650 --> 00:31:44.700
or something like that.

00:31:44.700 --> 00:31:45.810
What you're
essentially saying is

00:31:45.810 --> 00:31:47.640
that I'm going to sign
on with one provider

00:31:47.640 --> 00:31:49.920
and I'm going to get access
to multiple services.

00:31:49.920 --> 00:31:51.240
So the good part in this
is that, again, we're

00:31:51.240 --> 00:31:53.940
trusting Google to manage all
that extra work of checking

00:31:53.940 --> 00:31:56.820
devices, checking Security
Keys, checking passwords.

00:31:56.820 --> 00:31:58.700
And then eventually,
at the end, when

00:31:58.700 --> 00:32:01.980
the request comes in, we say,
is this guy who he says he is?

00:32:01.980 --> 00:32:04.185
And Google says, yeah,
I'm pretty confident this

00:32:04.185 --> 00:32:05.700
is who he says he is.

00:32:05.700 --> 00:32:08.670
Now, there's a few issues
that we can talk about this.

00:32:08.670 --> 00:32:11.300
I would like to have some
more, because right now,

00:32:11.300 --> 00:32:14.189
what we have is-- how many
of you guys use G Suite?

00:32:14.189 --> 00:32:15.480
So we've got a few people here.

00:32:15.480 --> 00:32:17.964
How many of you guys use
two-factor with your G Suite?

00:32:17.964 --> 00:32:19.380
So we've got a few
people that do.

00:32:19.380 --> 00:32:20.880
One of the things that
you'll find that G Suite does

00:32:20.880 --> 00:32:22.838
for us right now, which
is kind of interesting,

00:32:22.838 --> 00:32:28.567
is it assigns the level of trust
required to an actual user.

00:32:28.567 --> 00:32:30.150
And so what it's
going to say is, hey,

00:32:30.150 --> 00:32:32.700
Dale is a guy who
has access to quite

00:32:32.700 --> 00:32:36.630
a few top-secret
documents, and so we want

00:32:36.630 --> 00:32:38.064
to make sure he has two-factor.

00:32:38.064 --> 00:32:39.480
But then, what
that actually means

00:32:39.480 --> 00:32:44.010
for me is, now, when I want
to SSO into Stack Overflow,

00:32:44.010 --> 00:32:45.270
I need two-factor.

00:32:45.270 --> 00:32:47.100
And so one of the
things we would like

00:32:47.100 --> 00:32:50.580
is the ability to say, I
can, as an application,

00:32:50.580 --> 00:32:53.385
say what level of
trust I care about.

00:32:53.385 --> 00:32:55.740
And that could be either
at an application level,

00:32:55.740 --> 00:32:57.921
like IAP talks about,
or even within an app.

00:32:57.921 --> 00:32:59.670
Because you could
imagine, on Google Docs,

00:32:59.670 --> 00:33:02.340
it's frustrating if I have to
have Security Keys and a bunch

00:33:02.340 --> 00:33:03.960
of other stuff when I want
to read the Social Committee

00:33:03.960 --> 00:33:04.950
meeting minutes.

00:33:04.950 --> 00:33:09.355
And the same level of trust is
required for other documents.

00:33:09.355 --> 00:33:10.980
So some of that stuff,
I think, there's

00:33:10.980 --> 00:33:13.890
some room to move forward,
specifically around making

00:33:13.890 --> 00:33:15.550
it easier for these users.

00:33:15.550 --> 00:33:17.760
And so when I was talking
earlier about the service

00:33:17.760 --> 00:33:19.779
proxy, which is that
IAP, one of the things

00:33:19.779 --> 00:33:21.570
that's important to
see is that we actually

00:33:21.570 --> 00:33:23.580
use a couple pieces here.

00:33:23.580 --> 00:33:26.760
But a load balancer is obviously
another important component

00:33:26.760 --> 00:33:30.937
if your service has more
than one computer, or VM,

00:33:30.937 --> 00:33:33.270
or container, or whatever
that's going to be serving it.

00:33:33.270 --> 00:33:36.145
And so a Layer 7 load
balancer plus IAP

00:33:36.145 --> 00:33:38.520
is actually, in terms of if
you're reading the BeyondCorp

00:33:38.520 --> 00:33:40.650
paper, that's how we
view what the service

00:33:40.650 --> 00:33:42.660
proxy in that picture is.

00:33:42.660 --> 00:33:45.300
And this is what we use
in front of basically

00:33:45.300 --> 00:33:49.800
all of our services on
Kubernetes that we run in GKE.

00:33:49.800 --> 00:33:51.674
And so this is
what's implementing

00:33:51.674 --> 00:33:52.590
that particular piece.

00:33:52.590 --> 00:33:57.120
And I think I've got a beautiful
animation here that the Google

00:33:57.120 --> 00:33:59.190
marketing guys put together.

00:33:59.190 --> 00:34:01.990
This is what we were talking
about earlier, and then boom,

00:34:01.990 --> 00:34:04.040
it breaks that first
piece up into two.

00:34:06.960 --> 00:34:09.760
You guys have all probably
used the Layer 7 load balancer.

00:34:09.760 --> 00:34:10.802
It's really not that bad.

00:34:10.802 --> 00:34:12.718
And I mean, when Ameet
comes up here and shows

00:34:12.718 --> 00:34:15.429
that you just type in a policy
and it works out pretty well,

00:34:15.429 --> 00:34:18.690
this stuff, it is pretty
easy to get started with

00:34:18.690 --> 00:34:21.582
and add some pretty powerful
rules to add this security.

00:34:21.582 --> 00:34:23.040
And then, what
Cloud Endpoints does

00:34:23.040 --> 00:34:27.480
for us is, we implement our
microservices using gRPC.

00:34:27.480 --> 00:34:30.870
And what you'll find is
that a lot of our customers

00:34:30.870 --> 00:34:33.449
do not like or use gRPC.

00:34:33.449 --> 00:34:36.099
And for us, we don't
really want to have to--

00:34:36.099 --> 00:34:38.730
we don't have to lower our
performance or our expectations

00:34:38.730 --> 00:34:41.636
of our own APIs by implementing
them all as JSON REST.

00:34:41.636 --> 00:34:43.469
And so what we do is,
we use Cloud Endpoints

00:34:43.469 --> 00:34:44.684
to provide a JSON gateway.

00:34:44.684 --> 00:34:46.422
It provides monitoring.

00:34:46.422 --> 00:34:47.880
There's a lot of
great functionally

00:34:47.880 --> 00:34:50.088
the Cloud Endpoints provides,
and it's just a sidecar

00:34:50.088 --> 00:34:52.199
running along with our pods.

00:34:52.199 --> 00:34:54.105
And so that's what
we offer there.

00:34:54.105 --> 00:34:55.980
And so then, every single
one of the requests

00:34:55.980 --> 00:34:58.720
that's coming in here, we've got
an identity associated with it.

00:34:58.720 --> 00:35:00.390
We've got Cloud
Endpoints here to manage

00:35:00.390 --> 00:35:02.223
some of the monitoring
and the other things.

00:35:02.223 --> 00:35:05.070
And then our applications are
left to manage the access.

00:35:05.070 --> 00:35:08.190
And so, again, we have a
course-grain level from IAP,

00:35:08.190 --> 00:35:11.670
but again, we can implement
our own specific access

00:35:11.670 --> 00:35:15.096
requirements at
the service levels.

00:35:15.096 --> 00:35:17.220
I guess, one of the things
we look at here-- sorry,

00:35:17.220 --> 00:35:21.000
I guess I've got the
beautiful thing down here.

00:35:21.000 --> 00:35:24.060
We use these keys, again, to
protect accounts in our office.

00:35:24.060 --> 00:35:26.670
As an example, I have access
to delete GCP projects,

00:35:26.670 --> 00:35:29.460
and so for us, we
consider it important

00:35:29.460 --> 00:35:31.770
that I do use two-factor.

00:35:31.770 --> 00:35:34.980
Some of the stuff that I think
we were talking about earlier

00:35:34.980 --> 00:35:37.469
with Security Keys, this is
great that they're-- and I know

00:35:37.469 --> 00:35:39.510
a lot of people will say,
you can't say the words

00:35:39.510 --> 00:35:42.270
unphishable, which
I think is fair.

00:35:42.270 --> 00:35:44.760
I think we get into things
like the Security Keys

00:35:44.760 --> 00:35:47.746
actually on these guys
in the U2F standard.

00:35:47.746 --> 00:35:49.620
They actually support
client certs in the way

00:35:49.620 --> 00:35:50.980
that we were talking
about signing there,

00:35:50.980 --> 00:35:52.800
but you can actually
get down to a TLS level

00:35:52.800 --> 00:35:54.030
and do some of
those things, too.

00:35:54.030 --> 00:35:56.310
So it's pretty cool what
some of these things support.

00:35:56.310 --> 00:35:57.930
And it's important
to our business,

00:35:57.930 --> 00:35:59.680
because again, it's
not the small business

00:35:59.680 --> 00:36:01.830
that I have to necessarily
protect at this level,

00:36:01.830 --> 00:36:04.044
but it's basically the
people at my office

00:36:04.044 --> 00:36:06.210
that I need to make sure
this sort of stuff happens.

00:36:06.210 --> 00:36:08.730
But obviously, that only
works if I can actually

00:36:08.730 --> 00:36:12.660
delegate log-in into my
system for my users to Google.

00:36:12.660 --> 00:36:14.640
And so we've already done this.

00:36:14.640 --> 00:36:17.640
So what this means is, if you're
a user in any of my systems

00:36:17.640 --> 00:36:20.160
that has access to
administrative functionality,

00:36:20.160 --> 00:36:23.670
we actually require you to have
both the log-in into our system

00:36:23.670 --> 00:36:25.102
and a Google login.

00:36:25.102 --> 00:36:27.060
And so what it means is
that I can manage admin

00:36:27.060 --> 00:36:30.570
access to my entire
application through G Suite,

00:36:30.570 --> 00:36:33.012
because I can just disable
a G Suite account, and all

00:36:33.012 --> 00:36:34.470
of a sudden, now
you have no access

00:36:34.470 --> 00:36:36.720
to any more of the
admin functions.

00:36:36.720 --> 00:36:38.580
And then, in terms
of what we actually

00:36:38.580 --> 00:36:40.260
see as really exciting,
is our ability

00:36:40.260 --> 00:36:42.960
to throw in this second factor.

00:36:42.960 --> 00:36:46.230
And the one thing that we lack
right now as a part of this

00:36:46.230 --> 00:36:49.290
is when I get an identity,
I have no ability to say,

00:36:49.290 --> 00:36:51.780
hey, does this identity
have two-factor on it?

00:36:51.780 --> 00:36:54.390
So its up to me to
make sure that I've

00:36:54.390 --> 00:36:58.080
implemented two-factor on my
organization, and the functions

00:36:58.080 --> 00:37:00.240
that I want to be
two-factor-protected,

00:37:00.240 --> 00:37:01.660
that I've coordinated that.

00:37:01.660 --> 00:37:03.755
It would be really nice
if, when an identity came

00:37:03.755 --> 00:37:05.130
in, Google could
say, hey, here's

00:37:05.130 --> 00:37:06.934
the level of trust
associated with this,

00:37:06.934 --> 00:37:08.850
so I didn't have to make
sure that I've got it

00:37:08.850 --> 00:37:11.395
right in two different places.

00:37:11.395 --> 00:37:14.020
And so there's a whole bunch of
different things that I think--

00:37:14.020 --> 00:37:15.390
I want to summarize
a couple, I want

00:37:15.390 --> 00:37:17.265
to make sure I didn't
miss any of them here--

00:37:17.265 --> 00:37:19.980
but the idea of using
client certs, specifically

00:37:19.980 --> 00:37:20.770
to simplify.

00:37:20.770 --> 00:37:23.600
I think people have talked
about, at one of the talks

00:37:23.600 --> 00:37:27.630
I was at, it was, what version
of the best practice for OAuth

00:37:27.630 --> 00:37:28.950
should we be following?

00:37:28.950 --> 00:37:31.320
And in some ways, for
us, service to service is

00:37:31.320 --> 00:37:33.990
simplified if you just use
client-based certs in terms

00:37:33.990 --> 00:37:38.050
of, I have a client cert
that exists on one of these--

00:37:38.050 --> 00:37:39.810
either it's protected
in my service

00:37:39.810 --> 00:37:41.610
if it's a back-end
service, or maybe it's

00:37:41.610 --> 00:37:43.110
on my UB key or
something like that.

00:37:43.110 --> 00:37:44.818
It's something that
we would really like,

00:37:44.818 --> 00:37:46.455
and it's not something
that's supported

00:37:46.455 --> 00:37:49.080
right now, in terms of if you're
using a Layer 7 load balancer.

00:37:49.080 --> 00:37:52.600
So you can't actually do that
for some of these things.

00:37:52.600 --> 00:37:54.660
And then the other
thing that bothers

00:37:54.660 --> 00:37:58.001
us is, gRPC is not supported
right now on the Layer 7 load

00:37:58.001 --> 00:37:58.500
balancer.

00:37:58.500 --> 00:38:01.170
So while the picture up here
is showing a Layer 7 load

00:38:01.170 --> 00:38:03.210
balancer, we use that
for our JSON gateways.

00:38:03.210 --> 00:38:05.609
And the gRPC stuff goes through
a Layer 4 load balancer.

00:38:05.609 --> 00:38:07.650
So that kind of stuff is
something that we end up

00:38:07.650 --> 00:38:10.262
having to compromise in there.

00:38:10.262 --> 00:38:11.970
And then, I guess some
of the other stuff

00:38:11.970 --> 00:38:15.120
is, we would like
to see this ability

00:38:15.120 --> 00:38:18.000
to do more metadata
about the identity,

00:38:18.000 --> 00:38:19.860
because right now,
it's just, I get

00:38:19.860 --> 00:38:22.470
an email address, basically,
is what I get for an identity.

00:38:22.470 --> 00:38:24.180
And it would be nice
to support things,

00:38:24.180 --> 00:38:26.640
like we said, the metadata
about the level of trust,

00:38:26.640 --> 00:38:29.130
or even the case of,
again, I will call it

00:38:29.130 --> 00:38:32.930
a stacked identity, which
is, I'm microservice one,

00:38:32.930 --> 00:38:35.040
and I want to do this
work on behalf of Dale,

00:38:35.040 --> 00:38:38.370
and I actually have both of
those pieces of information

00:38:38.370 --> 00:38:39.480
that could be helpful.

00:38:39.480 --> 00:38:40.515
And so those are
some of the things

00:38:40.515 --> 00:38:42.600
that I'm looking at in
terms of our organization.

00:38:42.600 --> 00:38:43.650
And so again, what
you're going to find

00:38:43.650 --> 00:38:45.149
is that the conclusions
are actually

00:38:45.149 --> 00:38:46.950
very similar that we
came to that Ameet

00:38:46.950 --> 00:38:47.965
talked about earlier--

00:38:47.965 --> 00:38:48.840
or was it Christiaan?

00:38:48.840 --> 00:38:51.660
I think it was Christiaan that
actually summarized it up.

00:38:51.660 --> 00:38:54.480
We've implemented Security
Key enforcement internally,

00:38:54.480 --> 00:38:56.869
and we've implemented
Identity-Aware Proxy.

00:38:56.869 --> 00:38:58.410
And then what we've
specifically done

00:38:58.410 --> 00:39:01.770
is, we've moved to SSO for all
of our identity internally.

00:39:01.770 --> 00:39:03.540
And our goal is,
over the coming year,

00:39:03.540 --> 00:39:06.210
to start to roll more of that
out to our customers by adding

00:39:06.210 --> 00:39:09.840
support for SSO logins, and
then eventually removing

00:39:09.840 --> 00:39:12.862
support for username-
and password-based logins

00:39:12.862 --> 00:39:14.820
so that we can delegate
to trusted sources that

00:39:14.820 --> 00:39:18.540
have two-factor auth,
such as GitHub or Google.

00:39:18.540 --> 00:39:20.686
And so for our
end users, I guess

00:39:20.686 --> 00:39:22.560
we'll see what the
adoption rates are and see

00:39:22.560 --> 00:39:24.210
how many providers
we end up having

00:39:24.210 --> 00:39:26.820
to go with to make sure we
cover all the small businesses.

00:39:26.820 --> 00:39:29.100
But for internal,
since we have G Suite,

00:39:29.100 --> 00:39:31.140
and a large percentage
of our resellers do too,

00:39:31.140 --> 00:39:33.060
it's likely that we
will choose just Google

00:39:33.060 --> 00:39:35.550
to do our identity for that.

00:39:35.550 --> 00:39:37.080
So at this point,
I figured I'd ask

00:39:37.080 --> 00:39:40.374
if there's any questions
on some of this stuff.

00:39:40.374 --> 00:39:41.790
I find this stuff
really exciting.

00:39:41.790 --> 00:39:43.914
I find it interesting in
terms of reading about all

00:39:43.914 --> 00:39:47.430
this stuff and how these
standards around encryption

00:39:47.430 --> 00:39:50.100
work, and then not having
to implement half of them

00:39:50.100 --> 00:39:51.420
because Google does it for us.

00:39:51.420 --> 00:39:55.150
But I guess there's a
microphone here in the middle,

00:39:55.150 --> 00:39:59.500
so if anybody had any questions,
they'd be appreciated.

00:39:59.500 --> 00:40:02.200
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:05.800 --> 00:40:09.750
[MUSIC PLAYING]

